7
00:00:14.465 --> 00:00:19.009
Ch 09, Ch 19, Ch 24, What you will build:

8
00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:28.356
How the grocery platform's services are made fast and kept fast, measuring tails, profiling hotspots, choosing a GC, and planning capacity.

9
00:00:28.636 --> 00:00:36.487
Labs use real JFR recordings and real JVM garbage collectors on JDK 21 and 25.

10
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.452
L1: tail latency.

11
00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:47.372
At fan-out 100, ~99% of requests hit at least one slow backend, a backend's rare tail becomes the request's norm.

12
00:00:47.652 --> 00:00:56.569
On a real queue, wait time went 1 times, then 159 times from 50% to 99% utilization (the knee).

13
00:00:56.849 --> 00:01:06.279
And coordinated omission hid a 500 ms freeze (naive p999 0 ms, corrected 460 ms).

14
00:01:07.629 --> 00:01:11.405
L2: garbage collection, same workload across collectors.

15
00:01:11.685 --> 00:01:31.205
Parallel's worst pause was 118 ms, G1's 55 to 69 ms; generational ZGC on JDK 25 kept the request tail at max 7.5 ms or p999 3.4 ms while allocating the most (92 GB).

16
00:01:31.485 --> 00:01:32.999
L3: profiling.

17
00:01:33.279 --> 00:01:36.821
A real JFR recording located a planted hotspot:

18
00:01:37.101 --> 00:01:44.119
97% of allocation was byte from string-building, and the hot CPU frames rolled up to one method.

19
00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:50.138
The profile-guided fix ran 175 times faster with an identical checksum.

20
00:01:50.418 --> 00:01:51.826
L4: capacity.

21
00:01:52.106 --> 00:02:11.797
A real contended service scaled to ~10,000 req per second then plateaued; a Universal Scalability Law fit (α about 0.05) predicted the peak (~11,000 req per second at ~69 concurrent) from a handful of load points, and Little's law sized the pool.

22
00:02:13.147 --> 00:02:14.128
Reading time:

23
00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:16.328
120 min + code.

24
00:02:17.678 --> 00:02:18.531
Part 1.

25
00:02:18.811 --> 00:02:20.731
Introduction and Why It Matters.

26
00:02:21.981 --> 00:02:24.456
A story: the p99 that wouldn't budge.

27
00:02:24.736 --> 00:02:35.211
The product-page team spent a sprint optimizing the catalog service, caching, query tuning, faster serialization, and the page's p99 barely moved.

28
00:02:35.491 --> 00:02:40.696
The performance review found four separate misunderstandings, each a section of this chapter:

29
00:02:40.976 --> 00:02:42.768
They optimized the wrong service.

30
00:02:43.048 --> 00:02:50.920
A trace (Ch 24) showed the slow path ran through pricing, not catalog; catalog was never on the critical path.

31
00:02:51.200 --> 00:03:00.117
And because the page fanned out to ~20 services, the tail compounded: at that fan-out, most requests hit someone's slow tail (L1).

32
00:03:00.397 --> 00:03:04.493
Cutting catalog's median did nothing for the page's p99.

33
00:03:05.843 --> 00:03:07.763
A GC pause was half the tail.

34
00:03:08.043 --> 00:03:18.667
The service ran the Parallel collector with a 4 GB heap; its stop-the-world pauses reached 118 ms (L2), landing squarely in the p99.

35
00:03:18.947 --> 00:03:20.867
Nobody had looked at GC logs.

36
00:03:21.147 --> 00:03:22.534
Nobody had profiled.

37
00:03:22.814 --> 00:03:34.483
When they finally took a JFR recording, one method, building a cache key by string concatenation in a loop, accounted for almost all the allocation and most of the CPU (L3).

38
00:03:34.763 --> 00:03:39.073
A ten-minute fix gave a 175 times speedup on that method.

39
00:03:40.423 --> 00:03:42.087
The capacity plan was a guess.

40
00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:53.524
It handles 10k rps came from a load test that ran at fixed concurrency (so it couldn't overload the service) and measured latency wrong (coordinated omission, L1).

41
00:03:53.804 --> 00:04:02.913
The real knee was lower, and the service was already running past 80% utilization at peak, on the steep part of the queueing curve (L1 C).

42
00:04:03.193 --> 00:04:14.159
The rebuild was disciplined: measure the tail, trace to the critical path, profile before optimizing, read GC logs, and plan capacity with a model rather than a vibe.

43
00:04:15.509 --> 00:04:16.191
Quote:

44
00:04:16.471 --> 00:04:18.135
The thesis of this chapter.

45
00:04:18.415 --> 00:04:21.082
Performance work is measurement first, always.

46
00:04:21.362 --> 00:04:27.826
You cannot optimize what you haven't profiled, and intuition about where time goes is usually wrong (L3).

47
00:04:28.106 --> 00:04:36.298
Measure tails, not averages (Ch 24), because at scale the tail is what users feel and what fan-out amplifies (L1).

48
00:04:36.578 --> 00:04:43.639
Know your queueing curve, latency turns a corner near 100% utilization, so plan to run well below it.

49
00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:49.231
And treat the GC and the JVM as tunable parts of the system, not a black box.

50
