181
00:11:16.531 --> 00:11:17.513
The lessons:

182
00:11:17.793 --> 00:11:21.014
There's a knee around 70 to 80% utilization.

183
00:11:21.294 --> 00:11:26.798
Below it, latency is stable; above it, small load changes cause big latency jumps.

184
00:11:27.078 --> 00:11:37.062
Plan to run at ~60 to 70%, so a traffic spike or a lost instance (which raises everyone else's utilization) doesn't push you over the knee.

185
00:11:37.342 --> 00:11:43.955
Variability makes it worse: bursty arrivals or variable service times (a fat tail) push the knee lower.

186
00:11:44.235 --> 00:11:52.662
Little's law (L = λW, Ch 9) ties it together: in-flight work = arrival rate times time in system.

187
00:11:54.012 --> 00:11:54.929
Profiling:

188
00:11:55.209 --> 00:11:57.172
JFR and async-profiler.

189
00:11:57.452 --> 00:11:59.436
Don't optimize without a profile.

190
00:11:59.716 --> 00:12:00.676
The tools:

191
00:12:00.956 --> 00:12:08.252
JDK Flight Recorder (JFR): built into the JVM, ~1% overhead, always-on-capable.

192
00:12:08.532 --> 00:12:31.551
Records execution samples (CPU), allocation, locks, GC, I or O, and more, into a.jfr file you analyze with jfr print, JMC, or IntelliJ. async-profiler: a low-overhead sampling profiler that avoids the safepoint bias of older profilers and produces flame graphs for CPU, allocation, locks and cache misses.

193
00:12:32.901 --> 00:12:40.453
L3 recorded a real JFR profile of a workload with a planted hotspot and analyzed it with jfr print:

194
00:12:40.733 --> 00:12:55.602
CPU (execution samples): the hot self-time frames were Integer.getChars and ArraysSupport.mismatch, both rolling up to one method (3,139 of 3,159 samples).

195
00:12:55.882 --> 00:12:58.037
The profiler named the method to fix.

196
00:12:58.317 --> 00:12:59.703
Allocation samples:

197
00:12:59.983 --> 00:13:05.423
97% were byte, the signature of building strings by concatenation in a loop.

198
00:13:06.773 --> 00:13:25.845
The profile-guided fix (a StringBuilder plus a precomputed map instead of an O(n²) concat and a linear scan) ran 175 times faster (90,020 per second, then 15.8M per second) with an identical checksum, same behavior, vastly less work.

199
00:13:26.125 --> 00:13:41.144
Read a profile as: self time (where the CPU actually is) versus total time (inclusive), the top allocation sites (allocation pressure drives GC, §2.5), and lock contention (Ch 19).

200
00:13:41.424 --> 00:13:45.733
Flame graph width = time; look for wide frames you didn't expect.

