301
00:17:56.561 --> 00:18:07.164
\ ZGC's stop-the-world pauses are concurrent and sub-millisecond, so they don't appear as Pause... ms in the GC log; their effect is the request tail.

302
00:18:07.444 --> 00:18:08.297
Reading it:

303
00:18:08.577 --> 00:18:27.756
Parallel gives the classic few but long pauses (118 ms); G1 trades those for more but shorter; generational ZGC on JDK 25 both did the most work (92 GB allocated, most requests) and had the shortest tail (max 7.5 ms).

304
00:18:28.036 --> 00:18:31.364
For a latency-SLO service, that tail is the headline.

305
00:18:31.644 --> 00:18:34.716
For a nightly batch job, Parallel's throughput may win.

306
00:18:36.066 --> 00:18:38.071
Allocation is the real lever:

307
00:18:38.351 --> 00:18:42.490
GC work is proportional to allocation rate and live-set size.

308
00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:51.282
The cheapest GC tuning is allocating less (L3's fix), and the second is sizing the heap so the collector isn't constantly busy.

309
00:18:52.632 --> 00:18:54.104
GC tuning knobs.

310
00:18:54.384 --> 00:18:55.899
Pick the collector for the goal:

311
00:18:56.179 --> 00:19:02.173
ZGC or Shenandoah for latency, G1 for general, Parallel for throughput or batch.

312
00:19:02.453 --> 00:19:15.509
Heap size (-Xms = -Xmx to avoid resizing): big enough that GC isn't constant, not so big that a pause (on STW collectors) is huge or the container OOMs.

313
00:19:15.789 --> 00:19:25.048
G1: -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis (a target, not a guarantee); watch for humongous allocations and mixed-GC behavior.

314
00:19:26.398 --> 00:19:36.105
Observe, don't guess: -Xlog:gc (unified logging), JFR GC events, and pause or allocation-rate dashboards (Ch 24).

315
00:19:36.385 --> 00:19:41.377
Tune against measured pauses and allocation, never by copying flags from a blog.

316
00:19:41.657 --> 00:19:51.854
Beware full GCs (a fallback when concurrent collection can't keep up): they're long STW pauses and signal the heap is too small or allocation too high.

317
00:19:53.204 --> 00:19:54.697
JIT and warm-up.

318
00:19:54.977 --> 00:20:06.241
The JVM interprets bytecode first, then the JIT (C1 then C2) compiles hot methods to native code, so the first requests are much slower than steady state.

319
00:20:06.521 --> 00:20:07.588
Implications:

320
00:20:07.868 --> 00:20:14.567
Warm up before measuring (every lab here warms up first), a benchmark of cold code measures the interpreter.

321
00:20:14.847 --> 00:20:28.031
Production warm-up: send synthetic traffic before a new instance takes real load, or the first users after a deploy hit slow code (and a scale-up during a spike serves the spike on cold JITs).

322
00:20:29.381 --> 00:20:38.511
JMH for microbenchmarks (it handles warm-up, dead-code elimination and fork isolation), hand-rolled micro-timing is usually wrong.

323
00:20:38.791 --> 00:20:49.031
AOT or CRaC or GraalVM native image reduce warm-up for fast-start use cases (serverless), trading peak throughput or build complexity.

324
00:20:49.311 --> 00:20:53.322
Project Leyden is bringing more AOT to mainstream JDKs.

325
00:20:54.672 --> 00:20:56.357
The JVM in containers.

326
00:20:56.637 --> 00:21:02.056
Containers changed JVM defaults, and getting this wrong is a common performance bug:

327
00:21:02.336 --> 00:21:11.957
CPU: the JVM sizes GC threads, ForkJoinPool.commonPool and availableProcessors() from the container's CPU quota.

328
00:21:12.237 --> 00:21:15.544
A wrong or unset limit gives too few or too many threads.

329
00:21:15.824 --> 00:21:27.899
CPU limits (throttling) can cause latency spikes when the container is throttled mid-request; many teams set CPU requests but not hard limits for latency-sensitive services.

330
00:21:29.249 --> 00:21:39.787
Memory: use -XX:MaxRAMPercentage (for example, 75%) rather than a fixed -Xmx, so the heap tracks the container's memory limit.

331
00:21:40.067 --> 00:21:56.985
Leave headroom for non-heap memory (metaspace, thread stacks, direct buffers, code cache), the container limit must cover all JVM memory, or the kernel OOM-kills the process (exit 137), which looks like a crash, not a GC problem.

332
00:21:57.265 --> 00:22:08.913
Modern JDKs are container-aware (since 8u191 or 10 plus), but verify: check availableProcessors() and Runtime.maxMemory() inside the container.

333
00:22:10.263 --> 00:22:11.692
Load testing done right.

334
00:22:11.972 --> 00:22:13.679
Open versus closed model:

335
00:22:13.959 --> 00:22:23.025
Closed (fixed number of virtual users looping): in-flight is capped at N, so the test cannot overload the service, throughput just plateaus (L4).

336
00:22:23.305 --> 00:22:26.079
Good for finding a per-concurrency operating point.

337
00:22:26.359 --> 00:22:34.871
Open (fixed arrival rate, independent of responses): this is how real traffic behaves and the only way to see the overload cliff.

338
00:22:36.221 --> 00:22:39.421
Coordinated omission: the classic load-test bug.

339
00:22:39.701 --> 00:22:49.877
If the tool sends the next request only after the previous responds, then when the server stalls the tool stops sending, and never records the requests that would have been slow.

340
00:22:50.157 --> 00:23:01.677
L1 D: a 500 ms freeze showed as p999 = 0 ms naively, and 460 ms when measured from the scheduled send time.

341
00:23:01.957 --> 00:23:10.533
Fixes: measure latency from intended start time (wrk2, Gatling, k6 do this correctly), or use an open model.

342
00:23:11.883 --> 00:23:12.757
Tools:

343
00:23:13.037 --> 00:23:20.376
Gatling and k6 (open model, scripting), wrk2 (coordinated-omission-corrected), JMeter.

344
00:23:20.656 --> 00:23:28.315
Test against production-like data and dependencies; a load test against a warm cache and empty tables measures nothing.

345
00:23:28.595 --> 00:23:41.011
What to measure: throughput, latency percentiles (p50 or p99 or p99.9), error rate, and resource USE (Ch 24), and find the knee, not just one number.

346
00:23:42.361 --> 00:23:44.409
Capacity planning and the USL.

347
00:23:44.689 --> 00:23:46.395
Little's law (Ch 9):

348
00:23:46.675 --> 00:23:48.446
L = λ, W.

349
00:23:48.726 --> 00:23:56.491
To serve λ requests per second at W seconds each, you have L in flight, which sizes thread pools and connection pools.

350
00:23:56.771 --> 00:23:57.646
L4:

351
00:23:57.926 --> 00:24:05.521
2,000 rps times 1.1 ms about 2 in flight, so a pool of ~4 with headroom (not hundreds).

352
00:24:05.801 --> 00:24:11.731
Amdahl's law: a serial fraction α caps speedup at 1 or α no matter how many cores.

353
00:24:13.081 --> 00:24:15.449
Universal Scalability Law (USL):

354
00:24:15.729 --> 00:24:25.948
X(N) = N, X₁ / (1 + α(Nminus 1) + βN(Nminus 1)), adds a coherency term β (crosstalk between workers).

355
00:24:26.228 --> 00:24:32.116
Because of β, throughput doesn't just plateau, it can retrograde (fall) as concurrency rises.

356
00:24:32.396 --> 00:24:37.495
L4 fit the USL to a real contended service's measured throughput curve:

357
00:24:37.775 --> 00:24:46.884
Measured: scaled to ~10,000 req per second by N=16, then flat or slightly falling to N=256.

358
00:24:48.234 --> 00:25:06.069
Fit: α about 0.048 (the serialized critical section), β about 0.0002 (coherency), predicting a peak of ~11,000 req per second at ~69 concurrent, beyond which throughput retrogrades.

359
00:25:06.349 --> 00:25:21.090
The value: fit the model from a handful of load points and predict the peak, instead of testing to destruction, and it tells you why (contention versus coherency), pointing at the fix (shrink the serial section, Ch 19 or 20).

360
00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:36.431
Capacity plan: measure the per-instance peak, run at ~60 to 70% of it (queueing knee, L1 C), size instances for peak traffic plus headroom for a lost instance and a spike, and re-measure as the workload changes.

361
00:25:37.781 --> 00:25:39.936
A performance investigation method.

362
00:25:40.216 --> 00:25:45.933
Start from the SLO or symptom (Ch 24): which journey, which percentile, how bad.

363
00:25:46.213 --> 00:25:52.336
Trace to the critical path (Ch 24 L3): which service or span dominates the tail.

364
00:25:52.616 --> 00:25:56.093
Don't optimize off the critical path (§1.1).

365
00:25:56.373 --> 00:26:04.032
Profile that service (JFR or async-profiler, L3): find the hot method or allocation site by evidence.

366
00:26:04.312 --> 00:26:05.507
Check the platform:

367
00:26:05.787 --> 00:26:13.659
GC pauses (L2), CPU throttling, lock contention (Ch 19), connection-pool waits (Ch 19).

368
00:26:15.009 --> 00:26:17.035
Fix the biggest thing, then re-measure.

369
00:26:17.315 --> 00:26:20.643
One change at a time; performance is full of surprises.

370
00:26:20.923 --> 00:26:27.793
Guard it: a load test in CI (open model, percentiles) and a latency SLO so regressions page.

