533
00:35:46.663 --> 00:35:49.052
Why tail latency dominates at scale.

534
00:35:49.332 --> 00:36:00.660
A. fan-out amplification: how a service's p99 becomes a request's p50 once one. request touches N backends (Dean and Barroso, The Tail at Scale).

535
00:36:00.940 --> 00:36:02.967
B. hedged requests as the fix.

536
00:36:03.247 --> 00:36:04.463
C. queueing theory:

537
00:36:07.313 --> 00:36:08.230
On screen:

538
00:36:08.510 --> 00:36:09.129
A.

539
00:36:09.409 --> 00:36:19.713
Fan-out amplification, one slow backend makes the whole request slow. a single backend call: p50 10 ms, p95 112 ms,

540
00:36:19.993 --> 00:37:07.133
p99 271 ms (5% of calls are slow). request that waits for ALL of N backends p50 p95 p99. fan-out 1 10 ms 108 ms 270 ms (P≥1 slow = 5%). fan-out 5 17 ms 270 ms 303 ms (P≥1 slow = 23%). fan-out 20 174 ms 301 ms 310 ms (P≥1 slow = 64%). fan-out 100 283 ms 310 ms 316 ms (P≥1 slow = 99%). then at

541
00:37:07.413 --> 00:37:16.181
fan-out 100, ~99% of requests hit at least one slow backend: the backend's. rare tail becomes the request's NORM.

542
00:37:16.461 --> 00:37:25.165
This is why one slow dependency (Ch 24 L3) wrecks a fan-out page, and why you. must cut the tail, not the average.

543
00:37:25.445 --> 00:37:26.128
B.

544
00:37:26.408 --> 00:37:32.168
Hedging: send a 2nd request if the 1st is slow, take the first to answer (Ch 17).

545
00:37:32.448 --> 00:37:33.963
The rest is in the chapter.

546
00:37:36.813 --> 00:37:37.666
Reading it:

547
00:37:37.946 --> 00:37:47.866
A, compounding: a backend slow 5% of the time becomes, across a fan-out of 100, a request slow ~99% of the time.

548
00:37:48.146 --> 00:37:51.730
The request's median approaches the backend's p99.

549
00:37:52.010 --> 00:37:57.599
This is why §1.1's catalog optimization didn't help the fan-out page.

550
00:37:57.879 --> 00:38:14.242
B, hedging: a capped hedge (only when the first call exceeds p95) cut fan-out-20 p99 from 310 ms to 126 ms for 5% extra load, the tail-cutting technique from Ch 17.

551
00:38:15.592 --> 00:38:27.539
C, the queueing knee: measured wait time tracked the M or M or 1 formula and went 1 times, then 159 times from 50% to 99% utilization.

552
00:38:27.819 --> 00:38:31.083
There's a corner near 80%; plan to run below it.

553
00:38:31.363 --> 00:38:48.387
D, coordinated omission: a 500 ms freeze was invisible to a naive per-call measurement (p999 about 0 ms) and obvious when measured from the scheduled send time (p999 460 ms).

554
00:38:48.667 --> 00:38:52.123
Load tests that back off during a stall under-report the tail.

