640
00:48:34.515 --> 00:48:35.369
Reading it:

641
00:48:35.649 --> 00:48:49.003
The curve: throughput scales nearly linearly while there's slack, then plateaus around 10,000 req per second as the lock serializes the critical sections; latency and in-flight count (Little's law) climb with N.

642
00:48:49.283 --> 00:49:10.019
USL fit: from a handful of load points the model recovers α about 0.05 (the serialized section) and β about 0.0002 (coherency), and predicts the peak (~11,000 req per second at ~69 concurrent) beyond which throughput retrogrades.

643
00:49:10.299 --> 00:49:18.747
That lets you predict the ceiling without testing to destruction, and it points at the fix, shrink the serial section (Ch 19 or 20).

644
00:49:20.097 --> 00:49:21.740
Little's law sizes the pool:

645
00:49:22.020 --> 00:49:29.316
2,000 rps times ~1.1 ms about 2 in flight, then a pool of ~4, not hundreds.

646
00:49:29.596 --> 00:49:37.297
Closed versus open: this closed test caps in-flight at N, so it can only find operating points, never the overload cliff.

647
00:49:37.577 --> 00:49:46.751
An open model (fixed arrival rate, measured from the scheduled time) is what reveals the cliff and avoids coordinated omission (L1 D).

