00:52:29.109 --> 00:52:32.352
Open model or wrk2-style correction.

694
00:52:32.632 --> 00:52:33.848
Running past the knee.

695
00:52:34.128 --> 00:52:34.939
Symptom:

696
00:52:35.219 --> 00:52:38.013
Latency cliff on a small spike (L1 C).

697
00:52:38.293 --> 00:52:39.061
Cause:

698
00:52:39.341 --> 00:52:41.411
Utilization ~80%.

699
00:52:41.691 --> 00:52:42.693
Mitigation:

700
00:52:42.973 --> 00:52:46.045
Plan at most 70%, autoscale below the knee.

701
00:52:47.395 --> 00:52:48.803
CPU throttling.

702
00:52:49.083 --> 00:52:52.070
Symptom: p99 spikes unrelated to load.

703
00:52:52.350 --> 00:52:53.118
Cause:

704
00:52:53.398 --> 00:52:55.318
Container CPU hard limits.

705
00:52:55.598 --> 00:52:56.601
Mitigation:

706
00:52:56.881 --> 00:52:59.654
Tune or remove limits for latency services.

707
00:52:59.934 --> 00:53:02.409
OOM-kill (exit 137).

708
00:53:02.689 --> 00:53:03.499
Symptom:

709
00:53:03.779 --> 00:53:05.209
Crash with no exception.

710
00:53:05.489 --> 00:53:06.257
Cause:

711
00:53:06.537 --> 00:53:08.542
Heap + non-heap container limit.

712
00:53:08.822 --> 00:53:09.825
Mitigation:

713
00:53:10.105 --> 00:53:13.027
MaxRAMPercentage, headroom for off-heap.

714
00:53:13.307 --> 00:53:15.910
Cold-JIT after deploy or scale.

715
00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:17.001
Symptom:

716
00:53:17.281 --> 00:53:19.862
First requests slow, spikes on scale-up.

717
00:53:20.142 --> 00:53:20.910
Cause:

718
00:53:21.190 --> 00:53:22.107
No warm-up.

719
00:53:22.387 --> 00:53:23.390
Mitigation:

720
00:53:23.670 --> 00:53:26.123
Pre-warm instances before real traffic.

721
00:53:26.403 --> 00:53:27.897
Microbenchmark lies.

722
00:53:28.177 --> 00:53:28.987
Symptom:

723
00:53:29.267 --> 00:53:30.953
It's fast that isn't in prod.

724
00:53:31.233 --> 00:53:32.001
Cause:

725
00:53:32.281 --> 00:53:34.563
No warm-up or dead-code elimination.

726
00:53:34.843 --> 00:53:35.846
Mitigation:

727
00:53:36.126 --> 00:53:38.707
JMH; measure in a realistic harness.

728
00:53:40.057 --> 00:53:42.276
Observability (Ch 24).

729
00:53:42.556 --> 00:53:47.505
Latency percentiles per journey (never averages), with SLO burn alerts.

730
00:53:47.785 --> 00:53:55.529
GC metrics: pause time and count, allocation rate, heap occupancy, time-in-GC; alert on pause regressions.

731
00:53:55.809 --> 00:53:57.345
JVM or runtime:

732
00:53:57.625 --> 00:54:04.217
CPU throttling, thread states (Ch 19), safepoint time, code-cache and metaspace usage.

733
00:54:04.497 --> 00:54:10.001
Continuous JFR retrievable per incident; async-profiler flame graphs on demand.

734
00:54:10.281 --> 00:54:14.953
Load-test results tracked over time so a regression is visible before release.

735
00:54:16.303 --> 00:54:17.242
Security.

736
00:54:17.522 --> 00:54:23.367
Profiles and heap dumps can contain secrets and PII (in-memory tokens, customer data).

737
00:54:23.647 --> 00:54:32.309
Treat.jfr files and heap dumps as sensitive: access-controlled, redacted where possible, short retention (Ch 23).

738
00:54:32.589 --> 00:54:44.215
Don't expose profiling or diagnostic endpoints (JMX, jcmd over a socket, actuator heapdump) unauthenticated, they leak internals and can enable DoS.

739
00:54:44.495 --> 00:54:55.930
Performance features are DoS surfaces: an unbounded expensive query or a hedge that doubles load under stress can amplify an attack; cap and rate-limit (Ch 18).

740
00:54:57.280 --> 00:54:58.944
Performance and tuning knobs.

741
00:54:59.224 --> 00:55:00.568
Here is a quick table:

742
00:55:00.848 --> 00:55:02.064
Knob, and Guidance.

743
00:55:02.344 --> 00:55:03.304
GC:

744
00:55:03.584 --> 00:55:08.405
ZGC or Shenandoah for latency, G1 general, Parallel batch.

745
00:55:08.685 --> 00:55:17.027
Heap: -Xms=-Xmx or MaxRAMPercentage; big enough to avoid constant GC, within the container limit.

746
00:55:17.307 --> 00:55:18.843
CPU (containers):

747
00:55:19.123 --> 00:55:25.032
Correct requests; avoid hard limits for latency services; verify availableProcessors().

748
00:55:25.312 --> 00:55:26.101
Hedging:

749
00:55:26.381 --> 00:55:31.715
Only above p95, capped, idempotent calls (Ch 17 or 20).

750
00:55:31.995 --> 00:55:36.453
Utilization target: at most 70% of measured peak (queueing knee).

751
00:55:36.733 --> 00:55:37.693
Load model:

752
00:55:37.973 --> 00:55:40.832
Open, percentile-based, production-like data.

753
00:55:41.112 --> 00:55:41.880
Warm-up:

754
00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:45.723
Pre-warm on deploy and scale-up; JMH for micro.

755
00:55:47.073 --> 00:55:47.798
Cost.

756
00:55:48.078 --> 00:56:03.929
Latency and cost trade off through utilization: running at 50% is safe but doubles instance count; running at 95% is cheap until the knee bites (L1 C). ~60 to 70% is the usual sweet spot.

757
00:56:04.209 --> 00:56:15.921
The cheapest capacity is code that allocates and locks less (L3 gave 175 times; Ch 19 removes contention), often far cheaper than more instances.

758
00:56:16.201 --> 00:56:23.966
Right-size the JVM: an oversized heap wastes memory you pay for; an undersized one burns CPU in GC.

759
00:56:24.246 --> 00:56:24.993
Measure.

760
00:56:25.273 --> 00:56:35.043
Profiling and load testing cost engineering time but prevent the expensive outages (§1.1) and the throw instances at it reflex.

761
00:56:36.393 --> 00:56:37.332
Part 7.

762
00:56:37.612 --> 00:56:39.319
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

763
00:56:40.569 --> 00:56:41.209
1.

764
00:56:41.489 --> 00:56:43.323
Optimizing without profiling.

765
00:56:44.673 --> 00:56:57.004
Here is the Java code. before: the loop must be slow, rewrite the arithmetic. after: profile first, the JFR shows it's string-building and a linear scan (L3), not the arithmetic.

766
00:56:59.854 --> 00:57:00.558
2.

767
00:57:00.838 --> 00:57:04.273
Averages instead of tails (Ch 24, L1).

768
00:57:04.553 --> 00:57:08.691
A healthy average hides the p99 that fan-out amplifies.

769
00:57:08.971 --> 00:57:10.230
Track percentiles.

770
00:57:10.510 --> 00:57:11.257
3.

771
00:57:11.537 --> 00:57:14.929
Optimizing off the critical path (§1.1).

772
00:57:15.209 --> 00:57:20.137
Trace to find what actually determines the request's latency before touching code.

773
00:57:20.417 --> 00:57:21.121
4.

774
00:57:21.401 --> 00:57:22.894
Ignoring GC logs.

775
00:57:24.244 --> 00:57:38.900
Here is the shell code. before: no GC visibility; a 118 ms pause sits in the p99 unnoticed (L2). after: log and watch GC; pick a low-pause collector for latency services.

776
00:57:41.750 --> 00:57:42.518
5.

777
00:57:42.798 --> 00:57:44.675
Fixed -Xmx in a container.

778
00:57:46.025 --> 00:57:57.929
Here is the shell code. before: -Xmx3g in a pod whose limit later changes to 2Gi, then OOM-kill (137). after: track the limit, leave off-heap headroom.

779
00:58:00.779 --> 00:58:01.526
6.

780
00:58:01.806 --> 00:58:05.219
CPU hard limits on latency-sensitive services.

781
00:58:05.499 --> 00:58:10.065
Throttling mid-request causes p99 spikes unrelated to load.

782
00:58:10.345 --> 00:58:14.675
Prefer requests without hard caps for those services, and measure throttling.

783
00:58:14.955 --> 00:58:15.702
7.

784
00:58:15.982 --> 00:58:20.761
Closed-model load tests or coordinated omission (L1 D, L4).

785
00:58:22.111 --> 00:58:23.519
On screen: before:

786
00:58:23.799 --> 00:58:29.708
50 VUs looping send, then wait, then send; a stall stops sending, so the tail vanishes.

787
00:58:29.988 --> 00:58:41.807
(p999 about 0). after: open model at a fixed arrival rate, latency measured from the scheduled send. time (k6 or Gatling or wrk2).

788
00:58:44.657 --> 00:58:45.339
8.

789
00:58:45.619 --> 00:58:47.134
Benchmarking cold code.

790
00:58:47.414 --> 00:58:51.894
The JIT hasn't compiled hot methods yet; you're timing the interpreter.

791
00:58:52.174 --> 00:58:53.923
Warm up, or use JMH.

792
00:58:54.203 --> 00:58:54.950
9.

793
00:58:55.230 --> 00:58:56.595
Hedging without a cap.

794
00:58:56.875 --> 00:59:03.403
A retry or hedge on every request doubles downstream load and can cause the overload it was meant to avoid.

795
00:59:03.683 --> 00:59:07.907
Hedge only above p95, capped (L1 B, Ch 18).

796
00:59:08.187 --> 00:59:08.891
10.

797
00:59:09.171 --> 00:59:10.750
Running hot to save money.

798
00:59:11.030 --> 00:59:16.982
90%+ utilization looks efficient until a spike hits the queueing cliff (L1 C).

799
00:59:17.262 --> 00:59:18.435
Plan for headroom.

800
00:59:19.785 --> 00:59:20.617
11.

801
00:59:20.897 --> 00:59:22.455
Premature optimization.

802
00:59:22.735 --> 00:59:30.500
Complicating code for a path that isn't hot (the profile shows it's 0.1% of time) adds bugs for no gain.

803
00:59:30.780 --> 00:59:33.980
Optimize what the profile and the SLO say matters.

804
00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:35.049
12.

805
00:59:35.329 --> 00:59:39.340
String concatenation in loops or needless allocation (L3).

806
00:59:39.620 --> 00:59:45.636
Use StringBuilder, reuse buffers, avoid boxing in hot loops; allocation is GC pressure.

807
00:59:46.986 --> 00:59:47.861
Part 8.

808
00:59:48.141 --> 00:59:49.485
Interview Q and A.

809
00:59:50.735 --> 00:59:51.567
Beginner.

810
00:59:51.847 --> 00:59:52.871
Question 1.

811
00:59:53.151 --> 00:59:56.351
Why measure p99, not the average latency?

812
00:59:56.631 --> 00:59:57.996
Thirty-second answer:

813
00:59:58.276 --> 00:59:59.812
The average hides the tail.

814
01:00:00.092 --> 01:00:19.164
Most requests can be fast while a slice are terrible, and the average looks fine, but those slow requests are real users, and in a fan-out system they're amplified. p99 or p99.9 show what the worst-served users experience, which is what SLOs and user perception track.

815
01:00:20.514 --> 01:00:21.517
Question 2.

816
01:00:21.797 --> 01:00:25.039
What is a profiler and why use one before optimizing?

817
01:00:25.319 --> 01:00:26.685
Thirty-second answer:

818
01:00:26.965 --> 01:00:37.845
A profiler samples where the program actually spends time (CPU) and allocates memory, so you optimize by evidence instead of intuition, which is usually wrong.

819
01:00:38.125 --> 01:00:45.293
In Java, JFR (built in, ~1% overhead) and async-profiler (flame graphs) are the standard tools.

820
01:00:46.643 --> 01:00:47.688
Question 3.

821
01:00:47.968 --> 01:00:51.211
What does the garbage collector do, and why do pauses matter?

822
01:00:51.491 --> 01:00:52.856
Thirty-second answer:

823
01:00:53.136 --> 01:00:57.467
The GC reclaims memory no longer referenced, so you don't free manually.

824
01:00:57.747 --> 01:01:07.773
Some collectors stop the application (stop-the-world) to do part of that work; those pauses add directly to request latency and land in the p99.

825
01:01:08.053 --> 01:01:11.125
Which collector you choose sets how long those pauses are.

826
01:01:12.475 --> 01:01:13.542
Question 4.

827
01:01:13.822 --> 01:01:16.659
Why does latency spike as a server gets busy?

828
01:01:16.939 --> 01:01:18.305
Thirty-second answer:

829
01:01:18.585 --> 01:01:19.886
A server is a queue.

830
01:01:20.166 --> 01:01:30.406
As utilization rises, requests wait behind others, and wait time grows non-linearly, near 100% it explodes (S, ρ/(1minus ρ)).

831
01:01:30.686 --> 01:01:39.774
So a server at 95% has far worse latency than at 70%, and a small traffic increase there causes a big latency jump.

832
01:01:41.124 --> 01:01:42.127
Intermediate.

833
01:01:42.407 --> 01:01:43.516
Question 5.

834
01:01:43.796 --> 01:01:47.657
Explain tail-at-scale and how to reduce a fan-out request's tail.

835
01:01:47.937 --> 01:01:49.303
Thirty-second answer:

836
01:01:49.583 --> 01:01:58.329
When a request fans out to N services and waits for all, the slowest sets the latency, so the request's tail is worse than any single service's.

837
01:01:58.609 --> 01:02:10.535
Reduce it by: fewer or coarser calls (less fan-out), tight timeouts, hedged or backup requests for the slow ones, caching, and fixing the specific slow dependency found via tracing.

838
01:02:11.885 --> 01:02:12.994
Question 6.

839
01:02:13.274 --> 01:02:15.919
How do you profile a JVM in production?

840
01:02:16.199 --> 01:02:17.565
Thirty-second answer:

841
01:02:17.845 --> 01:02:30.986
Run JDK Flight Recorder continuously (low overhead) with a profiling settings template, and pull the.jfr when you need it, analyze in JMC or IntelliJ or with jfr print.

842
01:02:31.266 --> 01:02:38.306
For a live flame graph, attach async-profiler to the PID for CPU, allocation or lock profiling.

843
01:02:38.586 --> 01:02:42.277
Correlate with the SLO or trace to profile the right service.

844
01:02:43.627 --> 01:02:44.715
Question 7.

845
01:02:44.995 --> 01:02:49.389
Which GC would you choose for a latency-sensitive service, and how do you tune it?

846
01:02:49.669 --> 01:02:51.035
Thirty-second answer:

847
01:02:51.315 --> 01:03:03.603
A concurrent, low-pause collector, ZGC (generational since JDK 21, default in 25) or Shenandoah, so pauses stay sub-millisecond and out of the p99.

848
01:03:03.883 --> 01:03:14.357
Tune by sizing the heap (MaxRAMPercentage in containers) so GC isn't constant, logging GC (-Xlog:gc), and reducing allocation.

849
01:03:14.637 --> 01:03:18.136
For batch or throughput jobs, Parallel or G1 instead.

850
01:03:19.486 --> 01:03:20.510
Question 8.

851
01:03:20.790 --> 01:03:24.715
What is coordinated omission and why does it matter for load testing?

852
01:03:24.995 --> 01:03:26.361
Thirty-second answer:

853
01:03:26.641 --> 01:03:38.886
It's a measurement bug where a load tester, waiting for each response before sending the next, stops sending during a server stall, so the requests that would have been slow are never recorded, and the tail looks fine.

854
01:03:39.166 --> 01:03:43.049
It hides exactly the pauses you care about (GC, lock, freeze).

855
01:03:44.399 --> 01:03:45.465
Question 9.

856
01:03:45.745 --> 01:03:49.031
How does running the JVM in a container change things?

857
01:03:49.311 --> 01:03:50.676
Thirty-second answer:

858
01:03:50.956 --> 01:03:59.788
The JVM sizes the heap, GC threads and availableProcessors() from the container's limits, so wrong limits cause wrong behavior.

859
01:04:00.068 --> 01:04:11.097
Use MaxRAMPercentage so the heap tracks the memory limit, and leave headroom for non-heap memory or the kernel OOM-kills the process (exit 137).

860
01:04:11.377 --> 01:04:15.921
CPU hard limits can throttle mid-request and spike the p99.

861
01:04:17.271 --> 01:04:18.039
Senior.

862
01:04:18.319 --> 01:04:19.365
Question 10.

863
01:04:19.645 --> 01:04:23.037
Walk through diagnosing a latency regression end to end.

864
01:04:24.387 --> 01:04:25.517
Question 11.

865
01:04:25.797 --> 01:04:29.317
Explain the Universal Scalability Law and what you do with it.

866
01:04:29.597 --> 01:04:30.963
Thirty-second answer:

867
01:04:31.243 --> 01:04:47.648
USL models throughput versus concurrency as X(N) = N, X₁ / (1 + α(Nminus 1) + βN(Nminus 1)). α is contention (a serial section, Amdahl), β is coherency (crosstalk between workers).

868
01:04:47.928 --> 01:04:51.000
Because of β, throughput can peak and then retrograde.

869
01:04:51.280 --> 01:04:58.128
You fit it to a few measured load points and it predicts the peak and why, so you can plan capacity and target the fix.

870
01:04:59.478 --> 01:05:00.566
Question 12.

871
01:05:00.846 --> 01:05:04.195
How do you use Little's law to size pools and plan capacity?

872
01:05:04.475 --> 01:05:05.841
Thirty-second answer:

873
01:05:06.121 --> 01:05:12.115
Little's law: in-flight = arrival rate times time in system (L = λW).

874
01:05:12.395 --> 01:05:21.121
To serve λ requests per second at W each, you need L in flight, which sizes thread pools, connection pools and concurrency limits.

875
01:05:21.401 --> 01:05:29.187
Combined with the queueing knee, you target ~60 to 70% utilization and size instances for peak plus headroom.

876
01:05:30.537 --> 01:05:31.817
Question 13.

877
01:05:32.097 --> 01:05:34.956
The service allocates a lot and GC is busy.

878
01:05:35.236 --> 01:05:36.900
How do you reduce allocation?

879
01:05:37.180 --> 01:05:38.545
Thirty-second answer:

880
01:05:38.825 --> 01:05:58.985
Profile allocation (JFR ObjectAllocationSample) to find the sites, then: use StringBuilder instead of concatenation in loops, avoid autoboxing in hot paths, reuse buffers or objects (carefully), stream instead of materializing large collections, and choose primitive collections where it matters.

881
01:05:59.265 --> 01:06:02.039
Then size the heap so GC isn't constant.

882
01:06:03.389 --> 01:06:04.583
Question 14.

883
01:06:04.863 --> 01:06:07.061
How do you design a trustworthy load test?

884
01:06:07.341 --> 01:06:08.706
Thirty-second answer:

885
01:06:08.986 --> 01:06:29.146
Open model (fixed arrival rate, VUs scale so latency is measured, not throttled), corrected for coordinated omission (measure from the scheduled send time), against production-like data and dependencies, reporting p50 or p99 or p99.9 and error rate plus resource USE.

886
01:06:29.426 --> 01:06:34.738
Ramp to find the knee, not just one number, and run it in CI with a regression budget.

887
01:06:36.088 --> 01:06:36.984
Architect.

888
01:06:37.264 --> 01:06:38.459
Question 15.

889
01:06:38.739 --> 01:06:41.917
Design the performance-engineering practice for the platform.

890
01:06:43.267 --> 01:06:44.462
Question 16.

891
01:06:44.742 --> 01:06:49.329
Latency versus cost versus utilization: how do you decide where to run?

892
01:06:49.609 --> 01:06:50.974
Thirty-second answer:

893
01:06:51.254 --> 01:06:54.646
Utilization is the dial between cost and latency.

894
01:06:54.926 --> 01:07:04.377
Low utilization is safe but expensive (more instances); high utilization is cheap until the queueing knee, where latency explodes (L1 C).

895
01:07:04.657 --> 01:07:14.065
Pick a target (~60 to 70% of measured peak) that keeps you off the cliff with headroom for a spike and a lost node, then autoscale around it.

896
01:07:15.415 --> 01:07:16.759
Question 17.

897
01:07:17.039 --> 01:07:19.791
How do you keep performance from regressing over time?

898
01:07:20.071 --> 01:07:21.436
Thirty-second answer:

899
01:07:21.716 --> 01:07:40.041
Make it continuous and gated: latency SLOs with burn alerts (Ch 24), an open-model load test in CI with a percentile regression budget that fails the build, continuous profiling so you can compare before or after, and GC or allocation dashboards.

900
01:07:40.321 --> 01:07:45.036
Track per-instance capacity over releases so creeping regressions are visible.

901
01:07:46.386 --> 01:07:47.559
Question 18.

902
01:07:47.839 --> 01:07:52.597
When is the JVM the wrong tool, or when do you reach for native or AOT?

903
01:07:52.877 --> 01:07:54.242
Thirty-second answer:

904
01:07:54.522 --> 01:08:02.778
The JVM's warm-up and footprint hurt for very short-lived processes (serverless, CLIs) and tight-memory environments.

905
01:08:03.058 --> 01:08:12.679
There, GraalVM native image or CRaC (checkpoint or restore) give fast start and low footprint, trading peak throughput and build complexity.

906
01:08:12.959 --> 01:08:20.959
For long-running services, the JIT's peak performance usually wins, so you keep the JVM and fix warm-up with pre-warming.

907
01:08:22.309 --> 01:08:23.547
Question 19.

908
01:08:23.827 --> 01:08:26.920
A service is fast in staging and slow in production.

909
01:08:27.200 --> 01:08:29.077
What's your differential diagnosis?

910
01:08:29.357 --> 01:08:30.723
Thirty-second answer:

911
01:08:31.003 --> 01:08:48.880
Enumerate what differs: data size (staging has small tables, then different query plans, cache hit rates), concurrency or load (queueing knee, lock contention, GC pressure only appear under real load), hardware or containers (CPU limits, noisy neighbors, throttling),

912
01:08:49.160 --> 01:08:56.221
network or dependencies (real latency and tails, fan-out), and warm-up or JIT on fresh instances.

913
01:08:56.501 --> 01:09:03.371
Reproduce with production-like data and an open-model load test; compare JFR and GC between the two.

914
01:09:04.721 --> 01:09:05.830
Question 20.

915
01:09:06.110 --> 01:09:10.398
Where does performance engineering meet the rest of the system design in this book?

916
01:09:10.678 --> 01:09:12.043
Thirty-second answer:

917
01:09:12.323 --> 01:09:28.921
It's the measurement layer over everything: it validates the concurrency model (Ch 19), the caching (Ch 07), the resilience limits (Ch 18), the data access (Ch 05 or 21), and the SLOs (Ch 24).

918
01:09:29.201 --> 01:09:46.118
